</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">----<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegated Credentials for TLS<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0draft-ietf-tls-subcerts-09<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[...]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">1.=C2=A0 Introduction<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Typically, a TLS server uses a certi=
ficate provided by some entity<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0other than the operator of the serve=
r (a &quot;Certification Authority&quot; or<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0CA) [RFC8446] [RFC5280].=C2=A0 This =
organizational separation makes the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0TLS server operator dependent on the=
 CA for some aspects of its<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0operations, for example:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0* =C2=A0Whenever the server operator=
 wants to deploy a new certificate, it<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 has to interact with the CA.=
<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0* =C2=A0The server operator can only=
 use TLS signature schemes for which<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 the CA will issue credential=
s.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0These dependencies cause problems in=
 practice.=C2=A0 Server operators<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0often deploy TLS termination service=
s in locations such as remote<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0data centers or Content Delivery Net=
works (CDNs) where it may be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0difficult to detect key compromises.=
=C2=A0 Short-lived certificates may be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0used to limit the exposure of keys i=
n these cases.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I believe it would be clearer to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">summarize the problem and link it to the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">use case. I would propose something<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">around:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">These dependencies cause problems in<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">practice, the management of key exposure<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">necessarily requires an interaction with<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">the CA.<span>=C2=A0</span><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Typically server operators....<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0However, short-lived certificates ne=
ed to be renewed more frequently<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0than long-lived certificates.=C2=A0 =
If an external CA is unable to issue a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0certificate in time to replace a dep=
loyed certificate, the server<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0would no longer be able to present a=
 valid certificate to clients.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0With short-lived certificates, there=
 is a smaller window of time to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0renew a certificates and therefore a=
 higher risk that an outage at a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0CA will negatively affect the uptime=
 of the service.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0To reduce the dependency on external=
 CAs, this document proposes a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0limited delegation mechanism that al=
lows a TLS peer to issue its own<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credentials within the scope of a ce=
rtificate issued by an external<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0CA.=C2=A0 These credentials only ena=
ble the recipient of the delegation to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0speak for names that the CA has auth=
orized.=C2=A0 For clarity, we will<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0refer to the certificate issued by t=
he CA as a &quot;certificate&quot;, or<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;delegation certificate&quot;, =
and the one issued by the operator as a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;delegated credential&quot; or =
&quot;DC&quot;.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">From the text it is unclear why the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">signature scheme appears to be a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">constraint as well how it does not opens<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">to some sort of downgrade attacks if<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">left to the operator. =C2=A0<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">3.=C2=A0 Solution Overview<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[...]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">3.1.=C2=A0 Rationale<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegated credentials present a bett=
er alternative than other<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0delegation mechanisms like proxy cer=
tificates [RFC3820] for several<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0reasons:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0* =C2=A0There is no change needed to=
 certificate validation at the PKI<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 layer.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0* =C2=A0X.509 semantics are very ric=
h.=C2=A0 This can cause unintended<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 consequences if a service ow=
ner creates a proxy certificate where<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 the properties differ from t=
he leaf certificate.=C2=A0 For this reason,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 delegated credentials have v=
ery restricted semantics that should<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 not conflict with X.509 sema=
ntics.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0* =C2=A0Proxy certificates rely on t=
he certificate path building process<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 to establish a binding betwe=
en the proxy certificate and the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 server certificate.=C2=A0 Si=
nce the certificate path building process<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 is not cryptographically pro=
tected, it is possible that a proxy<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 certificate could be bound t=
o another certificate with the same<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 public key, with different X=
.509 parameters.=C2=A0 Delegated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 credentials, which rely on a=
 cryptographic binding between the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 entire certificate and the d=
elegated credential, cannot.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0* =C2=A0Each delegated credential is=
 bound to a specific signature<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 algorithm that may be used t=
o sign the TLS handshake ([RFC8446]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Barnes, et al. =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
Expires 28 December 2020 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0[Page 6]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Internet-Draft =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegat=
ed Credentials for TLS =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0June 2020<b=
r>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 section 4.2.3).=C2=A0 This p=
revents them from being used with other,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 perhaps unintended signature=
 algorithms.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">It is not clear to me why there is a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&quot;may be used&quot;. I suppose it concerns th=
e<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">use of the DC not the algorithm but that<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">was confusing.<span>=C2=A0</span><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I also believe that the specific<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">signature algorithm to sign the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">delegated credential could be part of<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">the rational. =C2=A0<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">3.2.=C2=A0 Related Work<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Many of the use cases for delegated =
credentials can also be addressed<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0using purely server-side mechanisms =
that do not require changes to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0client behavior (e.g., a PKCS#11 int=
erface or a remote signing<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0mechanism [KEYLESS]).=C2=A0 These me=
chanisms, however, incur per-<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0transaction latency, since the front=
-end server has to interact with<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0a back-end server that holds a priva=
te key.=C2=A0 The mechanism proposed<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0in this document allows the delegati=
on to be done off-line, with no<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0per-transaction latency.=C2=A0 The f=
igure below compares the message flows<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0for these two mechanisms with TLS 1.=
3 [RFC8446].<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Remote key signing:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Client =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Front-End =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Back-End<br=
>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|----ClientHello---&gt;| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---ServerHello----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---Certificate----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 |&lt;---remote sign----&gt;|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---CertVerify-----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
... =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegated credentials:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Client =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Front-End =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Back-End<br=
>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 |&lt;--DC distribution-&gt;|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|----ClientHello---&gt;| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---ServerHello----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---Certificate----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|&lt;---CertVerify-----| =C2=
=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
... =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0| =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0|<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0These two mechanisms can be compleme=
ntary.=C2=A0 A server could use<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credentials for clients that support=
 them, while using [KEYLESS] to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0support legacy clients.<span>=C2=A0<=
/span><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I believe that this sentence does not<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">show any complementary as subcert and<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">KEYLESS are targeting different version<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">of TLS, so they can hardly be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">complementary.=C2=A0 However (and luckily)<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">LURK provides an extension for TLS 1.3<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[draft-mglt-lurk-tls13] which enable a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">complementary use of these mechanisms<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">these mechanisms. I believe that would<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">be good to indicate the reason they<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">complement each other which is that LURK<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">protects the credentials for its<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">operations. These operations could be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">performed in the scope of subcert or TLS<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">1.3.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Note also that in a related section it<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">also worth mentioning that credentials<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">may be managed in different ways and<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">KEYLESS represents an valuable way to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">protect and manage these credentials in<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">TLS 1.2. However, KEYLESS is known to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">presents some vulnerabilities (PFS,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">signing oracle) so the protection<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">remains limited while the LURK extension<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">for TLS 1.2 [draft-mglt-lurk-tls12]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">addressed these issues and as a result<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">should provide a better protection.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">The private key for a delegated credential<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0can be used in place of a certificat=
e private key, so it is important<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0that the Front-End and Back-End are =
parties that have a trusted<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0relationship.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Use of short-lived certificates with=
 automated certificate issuance,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0e.g., with Automated Certificate Man=
agment Environment (ACME)<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Management<span>=C2=A0</span><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0[RFC8555], reduces the risk of key c=
ompromise, but has several<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0limitations.=C2=A0 Specifically, it =
introduces an operationally-critical<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0dependency on an external party.=C2=
=A0 It also limits the types of<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Barnes, et al. =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
Expires 28 December 2020 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0[Page 7]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Internet-Draft =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegat=
ed Credentials for TLS =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0June 2020<b=
r>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0algorithms supported for TLS authent=
ication to those the CA is<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0willing to issue a certificate for.=
=C2=A0 Nonetheless, existing automated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0issuance APIs like ACME may be usefu=
l for provisioning delegated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credentials.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">4.=C2=A0 Delegated Credentials<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0While X.509 forbids end-entity certi=
ficates from being used as<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0issuers for other certificates, it i=
s valid to use them to issue<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0other signed objects as long as the =
certificate contains the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0digitalSignature KeyUsage ([RFC5280]=
 section 4.2.1.3).=C2=A0 We define a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0new signed object format that would =
encode only the semantics that<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0are needed for this application.=C2=
=A0 The credential has the following<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0structure:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 struct {<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 uint32 valid_time;<br=
>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 SignatureScheme expec=
ted_cert_verify_algorithm;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 opaque ASN1_subjectPu=
blicKeyInfo&lt;1..2^24-1&gt;;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 } Credential;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0valid_time: =C2=A0Time in seconds re=
lative to the beginning of the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 delegation certificate&#39;s=
 notBefore value after which the delegated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 credential is no longer vali=
d.=C2=A0 This MUST NOT exceed 7 days.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I believe the behavior of the &quot;verifying<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">peer&quot; should also be specified maybe<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">with a reference.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0expected_cert_verify_algorithm: =C2=
=A0The signature algorithm of the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 credential key pair, where t=
he type SignatureScheme is as defined<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 in [RFC8446].=C2=A0 This is =
expected to be the same as<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 CertificateVerify.algorithm =
sent by the server.=C2=A0 Only signature<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 algorithms allowed for use i=
n CertificateVerify messages are<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 allowed.=C2=A0 When using RS=
A, the public key MUST NOT use the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 rsaEncryption OID, as a resu=
lt, the following algorithms are not<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 allowed for use with delegat=
ed credentials: rsa_pss_rsae_sha256,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 rsa_pss_rsae_sha384, rsa_pss=
_rsae_sha512.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">It is unclear whether the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">expected_cert_verify_algorithm and<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">CertificateVerify.algorithm needs to be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">checked and what needs to be done in<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">case of mismatch (with the RSA caveat).<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I believe that should be clarified.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0ASN1_subjectPublicKeyInfo: =C2=A0The=
 credential&#39;s public key, a DER-<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 encoded [X.690] SubjectPubli=
cKeyInfo as defined in [RFC5280].<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0The delegated credential has the fol=
lowing structure:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 struct {<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 Credential cred;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 SignatureScheme algor=
ithm;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 opaque signature&lt;0=
..2^16-1&gt;;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 } DelegatedCredential;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0algorithm: =C2=A0The signature algor=
ithm used to verify<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 DelegatedCredential.signatur=
e.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I am wondering if any checks should be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">performed with the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">CertificateVerify.algorithm or if any<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">algorithm would be acceptable. Unless I<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">am missing something it seems a weak<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">algorithm can be used - assuming the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">registry contains such weak algorithms.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Barnes, et al. =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
Expires 28 December 2020 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0[Page 8]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Internet-Draft =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegat=
ed Credentials for TLS =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0June 2020<b=
r>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0signature: =C2=A0The delegation, a s=
ignature that binds the credential to<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 the end-entity certificate&#=
39;s public key as specified below.=C2=A0 The<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 signature scheme is specifie=
d by DelegatedCredential.algorithm.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[...]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">4.1.1.=C2=A0 Server Authentication<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0A client which supports this specifi=
cation SHALL send a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0&quot;delegated_credential&quot; ext=
ension in its ClientHello.=C2=A0 The body of the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0extension consists of a SignatureSch=
emeList:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Barnes, et al. =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0=
Expires 28 December 2020 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =
=C2=A0[Page 9]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">Internet-Draft =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Delegat=
ed Credentials for TLS =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0June 2020<b=
r>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 struct {<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 SignatureScheme suppo=
rted_signature_algorithm&lt;2..2^16-2&gt;;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 } SignatureSchemeList;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0If the client receives a delegated c=
redential without indicating<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0support, then the client MUST abort =
with an &quot;unexpected_message&quot;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0alert.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0If the extension is present, the ser=
ver MAY send a delegated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credential; if the extension is not =
present, the server MUST NOT send<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0a delegated credential.=C2=A0 The se=
rver MUST ignore the extension unless<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0TLS 1.3 or a later version is negoti=
ated.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0The server MUST send the delegated c=
redential as an extension in the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0CertificateEntry of its end-entity c=
ertificate; the client SHOULD<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0ignore delegated credentials sent as=
 extensions to any other<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0certificate.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0The expected_cert_verify_algorithm f=
ield MUST be of a type advertised<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0by the client in the SignatureScheme=
List and is considered invalid<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0otherwise.=C2=A0 Clients that receiv=
e invalid delegated credentials MUST<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0terminate the connection with an &qu=
ot;illegal_parameter&quot; alert.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I am wondering what would prevent any<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">downgrade attacks if the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">SignatureSchemeList and<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">signature_algorithms have two different<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">sets of lists. My current understanding<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">is that these extensions are handled<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">independently, but I might be missing<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">something.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I am wondering if that would be<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">appropriated to specify the signature of<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">the CertificateVerify depending on the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">presence of the delegated credential - I<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">mean the key used to generate it.<span>=C2=A0</sp=
an><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[...]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">4.1.3.=C2=A0 Validating a Delegated Credential<br=
>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0On receiving a delegated credential =
and a certificate chain, the peer<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0validates the certificate chain and =
matches the end-entity<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0certificate to the peer&#39;s expect=
ed identity.=C2=A0 It also takes the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0following steps:<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A01.=C2=A0 Verify that the current tim=
e is within the validity interval of<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0the credential.=C2=A0 =
This is done by asserting that the current time<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0is no more than the de=
legation certificate&#39;s notBefore value plus<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0DelegatedCredential.cr=
ed.valid_time.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A02.=C2=A0 Verify that the credential&=
#39;s remaining validity time is no more<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0than the maximum valid=
ity period.=C2=A0 This is done by asserting that<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0the current time is no=
 more than the delegation certificate&#39;s<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0notBefore value plus D=
elegatedCredential.cred.valid_time plus the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0maximum validity perio=
d.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A03.=C2=A0 Verify that expected_cert_v=
erify_algorithm matches the scheme<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0indicated in the peer&=
#39;s CertificateVerify message and that the<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0algorithm is allowed f=
or use with delegated credentials.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I am wondering if a reference to specify<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">what allowed would not be needed unless<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">it means advertised in the extension. =C2=A0<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A04.=C2=A0 Verify that the end-entity =
certificate satisfies the conditions<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0in Section 4.2.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A05.=C2=A0 Use the public key in the p=
eer&#39;s end-entity certificate to verify<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0the signature of the c=
redential using the algorithm indicated by<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0DelegatedCredential.al=
gorithm.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0If one or more of these checks fail,=
 then the delegated credential is<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0deemed invalid.=C2=A0 Clients and se=
rvers that receive invalid delegated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credentials MUST terminate the conne=
ction with an &quot;illegal_parameter&quot;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0alert.=C2=A0 If successful, the part=
icipant receiving the Certificate<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0message uses the public key in the c=
redential to verify the signature<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0in the peer&#39;s CertificateVerify =
message.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[...]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">7.=C2=A0 Security Considerations<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">[...]<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">7.6.=C2=A0 The Impact of Signature Forgery Attack=
s<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0When TLS 1.2 servers support RSA key=
 exchange, they may be vulnerable<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0to attacks that allow forging an RSA=
 signature over an arbitrary<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0message [BLEI].=C2=A0 TLS 1.2 [RFC52=
46] (Section 7.4.7.1.) describes a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0mitigation strategy requiring carefu=
l implementation of timing<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0resistant countermeasures for preven=
ting these attacks.=C2=A0 Experience<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0shows that in practice, server imple=
mentations may fail to fully stop<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0these attacks due to the complexity =
of this mitigation [ROBOT].=C2=A0 For<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0TLS 1.2 servers that support RSA key=
 exchange using a DC-enabled end-<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0entity certificate, a hypothetical s=
ignature forgery attack would<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0allow forging a signature over a del=
egated credential.=C2=A0 The forged<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0credential could then be used by the=
 attacker as the equivalent of a<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0man-in-the-middle certificate, valid=
 for 7 days.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">I do not see the relevance to TLS 1.3.<span>=C2=
=A0</span><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">&lt;/mglt&gt;<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Server operators should therefore mi=
nimize the risk of using DC-<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0enabled end-entity certificates wher=
e a signature forgery oracle may<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0be present.=C2=A0 If possible, serve=
r operators may choose to use DC-<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0enabled certificates only for signin=
g credentials, and not for<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0serving non-DC TLS traffic.=C2=A0 Fu=
rthermore, server operators may use<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0elliptic curve certificates for DC-e=
nabled traffic, while using RSA<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0certificates without the DelegationU=
sage certificate extension for<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0non-DC traffic; this completely prev=
ents such attacks.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0Note that if a signature can be forg=
ed over an arbitrary credential,<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0the attacker can choose any value fo=
r the valid_time field.=C2=A0 Repeated<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0signature forgeries therefore allow =
the attacker to create multiple<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0delegated credentials that can cover=
 the entire validity period of<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0the certificate.=C2=A0 Temporary exp=
osure of the key or a signing oracle<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0may allow the attacker to impersonat=
e a server for the lifetime of<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px">=C2=A0 =C2=A0the certificate.<br>
</div>
<div style=3D"margin:0px"><br style=3D"font-size:16px;background-color:rgb(=
255,255,255)">
</div>
</div>
<br>
</div>
